{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788326943,"nonce":"FgQckFMIbVQIezqhAHB2UTFQlAk=","message":"Run `m1-20260901` wiped at tick 3,948,132 (day 66, all six colonists dead). RimWorld offered its own recovery — the `GameEnded` letter with option **\"Create new wanderers\"**, which is the standing contingency Evan gave the run for exactly this case: *\"if your colony is over, start a new one if you can accept the new colony prompt that happens after like a day of everyone dead, if not close the game.\"*\n\n**The protocol cannot take it.**\n\n```\nletter-choose {index:2, option:1}   -\u003e  ok, chosen \"Create new wanderers\"\ninteractions                        -\u003e  force_pause 1, windows:\n                                        [{\"type\":\"Dialog_ChooseNewWanderers\",\n                                          \"fields\":[\"extraOnGUIProfilerLabelCached\",\n                                                    \"onGUIProfilerLabelCached\"]}]\ndialog-choose                       -\u003e  ok:false \"no Dialog_NodeTree is open\"\ndialog-dismiss                      -\u003e  ok:true, removed Dialog_ChooseNewWanderers\nadvance x6 (15,000 ticks each)      -\u003e  colonists = 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0\n```\n\n`Dialog_ChooseNewWanderers` is a **selection** window, not a node tree, so `dialog-choose` cannot address it. `dialog-dismiss` removes it and reports success — and **cancels the colony creation**. The `GameEnded` letter stays on the stack and re-choosing it re-opens the same unanswerable dialog, so the loop is closed: the game is offering a recovery the agent is structurally unable to accept.\n\nNote the `fields` scrape is empty of anything actionable this time — unlike `Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement`, whose `curName`/`curSecondName` at least *contained* a valid answer. Here there is nothing to accept; the window needs a selection made in its own UI.\n\n\nThe original filing was scoped as \"an unattended run stops for 1,000 ticks at a time\". This case is worse in kind:\n\n- **It is terminal.** A wedge can be unwedged by a human alt-tabbing once. This one has no protocol path at all, and the run ends there.\n- **It is the guaranteed endgame of every failed colony.** `GameEnded` is not an edge case — it is where every wipe lands, and \"survive as long as possible\" runs are *expected* to reach it. An agent that cannot answer it cannot chain colonies, which is the whole shape of a long-horizon run.\n- **`dialog-dismiss` is actively destructive here**, not merely inadequate. It consumed the offer. A verb that reports `removed: true` for an action that threw away the recovery is the same \"truthful field answering a different question\" failure as `253c694`'s silent order displacement.\n\n\n7. `Dialog_ChooseNewWanderers` is answerable: either a generic `dialog-accept` that confirms the window's current selection, or a specific route for the `GameEnded` recovery. Proving it: wipe a fixture colony, take the `GameEnded` option, assert colonists \u003e 0 within a few thousand ticks.\n8. **`dialog-dismiss` must refuse, or loudly warn, on a window whose dismissal destroys an offer** rather than reporting a bare `removed: true`. At minimum it should name what was discarded, the way `ignored_args` names a dropped argument.\n9. `PLAY-LOOP.md` gains the post-wipe branch: a wiped colony is not necessarily the end of a run, and the loop should know that `GameEnded` carries a recovery — today nothing in the playbook mentions it.\n\n\nClosed the game, per Evan's instruction. Colony `Gountown` ended with six dead and one colonist (Jimmy) kidnapped and never recovered; final save `RUNS/m1-20260901/saves/WIPE-t3948132.rws`.","files":null}]}